    "humanReview": "Qualitative user approval; numeric ratings remain unmeasured.",
    "solution": "Dash down-right toward the outside tally, and cut during the dash. The dash raises the shade over the beam and makes the western shore solid. Once the tally lights, jump left: the late reversal grace lets this follow the cut as a readable sequence. The spent launch tongue still disappears behind you. Hold left across the slightly wider receiving shore to the open gate.",
    "nearestExisting": "Replaces the two close, walkable shelves of the original F31. F32 also taught a low reverse hyper; this compact room makes the outward blade reach and the long inward speed crossing one continuous commitment.",
    "novelty": "The dash buys access to a key in the wrong direction, and the launch floor is spent before the return. The same dash must therefore become the leftward launch; walking out and returning to prepare a forward hyper loses the floor and meets the beam.",
    "hintTeaching": "Strike can now follow the ground dash instead of sharing its exact frame. Once the right tally lights, hold Left and press Jump; a short grace accepts the reversal even just after the dash ends.",
    "designNotes": "Second human review found the idea good but the action nearly impossible. The survive-2 profile now remembers a grounded dash for an eight-frame post-dash reverse-jump grace; stale outgoing wall momentum cannot overwrite the reversal. A ground-skimming dash also accepts a later Strike, removing the exact simultaneous button chord. Forward hyper speed, dash refill rules and unprofiled rooms are unchanged. Receiving shore extends one tile.All fourteen consecutive reversal delays 7\u201320 complete, and five offset Strike presses complete. Departure beam, remote tally and spent perch still physically enforce the outward commitment.",
